{"name":"Adrift on the Nile","hex":"#93b8e3","fetched":"2026-10-19","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"adrift","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In or into a drifting condition; moved (especially on water by waves and wind) without being propelled or steered.","Not in the expected or usual place or position; awry; also, not following the expected or usual course; astray, off course."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Drifting (especially on water) without being propelled or steered.","Of a person or group of people: having no aim or direction; aimless, lost."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/adrift"}],"entries":[{"title":"Adrift on the Nile","blocks":[{"text":"Adrift on the Nile is a 1966 book by Egyptian author and Nobel Laureate Naguib Mahfouz. The novel was later made into a 1971 film, Chitchat on the Nile. It was translated from Arabic into English in 1993 by Frances Liardet and published by Doubleday."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/52/Adrift_on_the_Nile_book.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adrift_on_the_Nile","wikidata":"Q16207487"},{"title":"Nile","blocks":[{"text":"The Nile is a major north-flowing river in northeast Africa which empties into the Mediterranean Sea. At 7,088 kilometers (4,404 mi) long, it is the longest river in the world, although the volume of water it carries is much smaller than other major rivers such as the Amazon or the Congo. The Nile has played a central role in the environmental, economic, and cultural history of Africa for millennia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg/330px-Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nile","wikidata":"Q3392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/52/Adrift_on_the_Nile_book.jpg/250px-Adrift_on_the_Nile_book.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/52/Adrift_on_the_Nile_book.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/52/Adrift_on_the_Nile_book.jpg/250px-Adrift_on_the_Nile_book.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdrift_on_the_Nile_book.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg/500px-Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg/1280px-Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg/500px-Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Whiteandblueniles.jpg/500px-Whiteandblueniles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Whiteandblueniles.jpg/500px-Whiteandblueniles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The distinct colors of the White Nile (left) and the Blue Nile (right) are visible where they merge to form the main Nile (top left).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhiteandblueniles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Ass